title: Teardown, verified against Azure rather than against the exit code
subtitle: week-14 — a failed teardown here costs about $13 a day
width: 1000
---
$ bash week-14-postgres-no-passwords/scripts/cleanup.sh

== figures first -- teardown is the only irreversible step in a week
  week-14-postgres-no-passwords: 8 slot(s) declared, 6 captured
  every declared figure is captured or retired -- safe to destroy.

== deleting the resource group   19:28:16Z

== terraform destroy (clears state of anything already gone)
Plan: 0 to add, 0 to change, 1 to destroy.
Destroy complete! Resources: 1 destroyed.
  destroy exit: 0  -- not trusted, see the counts below

== what Azure says is left   19:36:27Z
  PostgreSQL flexible servers        0
  virtual machines                   0
  managed disks                      0
  private endpoints                  0
  NAT gateways                       0
  public IP addresses                0
  private DNS zones                  0
  the resource group                 0

  clean. Nothing from this week is billing.

# The resource group goes first and whole, so the destroy finds 19 of the 20
# resources already gone and removes only the throwaway SSH key from state.
# That is why the counts are asked of Azure afterwards: a destroy can exit 0
# having done nothing, and week 05 left a firewall billing exactly that way.
#
# Deployed 18:21Z, destroyed 19:36Z -- a 1h15m window against a 3h budget.
